WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.780
Announcer: From the nation's capital!

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.960
The American Enterprise Institute for Public Policy Research presents

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.960
Public Policy Forums.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.720
A series of programs featuring the nation's top authorities presenting their differing views

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.220
on the vital issues which confront us.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.760
Today's topic arm sales, a useful foreign
policy tool?

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.880
John Charles Daly: An examination of arms sales
or arms transfers if you will as a tool of

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:41.050
foreign policy must inevitably recognize the
potential of such sales to affect the economic,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:47.480
military, psychological as well as the diplomatic
posture of the recipients.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:54.550
More in each of these areas the recipient's
posture plays a part large or small but always

00:00:54.550 --> 00:01:00.520
important in the grand design of US policy
to protect crucial American interests and

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:08.050
those of our allies in Western Europe, the
Middle East, Africa, Asia and South America.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:13.350
Our discussion of arms sales to be sure is
concerned solely with conventional arms, not

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:20.620
atomic weapons but a vital factor is the extraordinary
sophistication which has developed in conventional

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:28.230
arms in this electronic era and the potential
hazards to our own overall security if these

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:33.500
sophisticated elements are in the hands of
nations whose borders lie in the volatile

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.620
areas of confrontation between the interests
of the United States and her allies and the

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.700
Soviet Union.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:47.799
In mid-1981 the Reagan administration announced
its arms transfer policy emphasizing that

00:01:47.799 --> 00:01:57.119
the "basic thrust is to recognize that arms
transfers properly considered and employed

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:03.780
represent an indispensable instrument of American
policy that both complements and supplements

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:08.259
the role of our own military forces".

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:14.319
This is a marked change of direction from
Carter administration policy emphasizing restraint

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:20.639
and primary consideration of a recipient's
human rights policies in approving arms sales.

00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:29.010
But to examine the question mark on our arms
sales issue we have a highly expert panel.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:35.099
To my far right Lieutenant General Brent Scowcroft,
a West Point graduate who holds a doctorate

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:40.680
in international relations from Columbia University,
served with the Joint Chiefs of Staff and

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.819
after retiring from the service was assistant
to President Ford for national security affairs.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:52.440
To my immediate right, Representative William
L. Dickinson, a Republican from Alabama and

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.769
ranking minority member of the House Armed
Services Committee and Subcommittee on Research

00:02:57.769 --> 00:02:59.859
and Development.

00:02:59.859 --> 00:03:07.469
To my immediate left, Senator Joseph R. Biden
of Delaware, member of the Senate Foreign

00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:09.180
Relations Committee.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:15.150
Senator Biden served as chairman of the US
Senate Delegations on SOK2 in Moscow and to

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:20.419
the North Atlantic Assembly in Ottawa in 1979.

00:03:20.419 --> 00:03:26.340
To my far left, Representative Less Aspin,
six term Democrat from Wisconsin with a doctorate

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.889
in economics from MIT.

00:03:28.889 --> 00:03:33.780
Doctor Aspin earlier served as an economist
with the Pentagon and now is a member of the

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.459
Armed Services and Budget Committees of the
House.

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:43.010
Well, to begin gentlemen I would pose the
same question to each of you in turn.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:50.470
To what extent should an arms transfer or
sales program play a role in our foreign policy?

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:51.470
Senator Biden.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:56.069
Senator Biden: Well, it seems to me that we
have an expression, those of us who practiced

00:03:56.069 --> 00:04:01.260
law before coming to the Congress that hard
cases make bad law.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.889
And I think it's very difficult to make sweeping
statements and generalities about arms control

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:11.939
policy but to the extent that one must be
made it seems to me that the US should have

00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:18.099
a restrained...be both restrained and prudent
in making arms transfers and arms sales while

00:04:18.099 --> 00:04:23.080
at the same time recognizing that in particular
circumstances they can be very vital and important

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.810
to US interests.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:32.150
There are instances where arms transfers are
in fact absolutely essential for US interests

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:37.940
and others where we have made bad or incorrect
judgments about the stability of the nations

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:44.900
to whom we were transferring the arms and
failed in other instances to factor in the

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:49.940
arms transfer as part of the overall foreign
policy determination for a region or a country.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:51.990
So it's sort of a mixed bag.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.389
I think it's neither indispensable as this
administration says nor should it have been

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:01.620
an exceptional tool in our foreign policy
as the last administration indicated.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.060
John Charles Daly: Representative Dickinson.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:08.169
Representative Dickinson: I agree in the main...with
what the senator said.

00:05:08.169 --> 00:05:13.870
Whether we like it or not it is an instrument
of foreign policy and you cannot escape it.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:19.110
That being so, the best thing we could do
and what we should do is to use it as Senator

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.069
Biden said in a very prudent manner.

00:05:22.069 --> 00:05:31.040
But whether we are talking about our friends
and allies or potential adversaries we are

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:39.409
going to have to influence our foreign policy
with the use of weapon sales just as the Soviets

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:46.970
do, just as the French do and I don't advocate
an arms race but to say that it is not an

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:49.740
instrument of it is to ignore the obvious.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.259
John Charles Daly: Representative Aspin?

00:05:51.259 --> 00:05:52.601
Representative Aspin: I agree.

00:05:52.601 --> 00:05:57.130
I think that there's no question that it is
an instrument whether we like it or not.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:58.229
There it is.

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:04.590
What makes it so difficult is that there is
an ethical and a moral dimension to arms sales

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.440
as well as a national security dimension.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.460
It's not unlike human rights in that way.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.680
It's a complicated issue where you've got
ethics and morality competing with national

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.460
security considerations which means I think
two things.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:21.000
Number one, it's very difficult to take an
extreme position either totally for arms sales

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.729
or totally against and secondly, it's very,
very difficult to be totally consistent.

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:27.669
You may find two similar cases.

00:06:27.669 --> 00:06:30.490
In one case you wanna sell arms, in other
case you're not.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:33.439
All in all, it's a very difficult subject
to deal with.

00:06:33.439 --> 00:06:34.750
John Charles Daly: General Scowcroft.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:42.280
General Scowcroft: I think it's a rather important
as an instrument of US foreign policy.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.870
After all there are relatively few such instruments
that we have.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:53.120
We have diplomatic policy, economic policy,
economic aid and arms transfers if you will.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:58.410
Arms transfers are unique in the sense that
whether we like it or not the world in which

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:06.099
we live is one in which power is a primal
element.

00:07:06.099 --> 00:07:11.560
And where that is a case insecurity is also
an important element.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:18.840
And arms sales in addition to US military
policy itself, the US military guarantees

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:26.419
and so on serves an indispensable role in
lessening the kind of insecurity that the

00:07:26.419 --> 00:07:27.650
world in which we live in produces.

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:28.889
John Charles Daly: Right.

00:07:28.889 --> 00:07:33.849
We're examining a change in direction in arms
transfer policy.

00:07:33.849 --> 00:07:39.050
President Carter launched a restraint policy,
a unilateral effort to reduce arms transfers

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:45.279
to the Third World with a multibillion dollar
ceiling but a set of guidelines hopefully

00:07:45.279 --> 00:07:50.639
setting a good example to other arms suppliers
and he also had a multilateral effort launched

00:07:50.639 --> 00:07:55.909
in discussions with European nations and formal
negotiations with the Soviet Union.

00:07:55.909 --> 00:08:03.520
Even earlier the Congress had restricted arms
transfers and assistance personnel by numbers,

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.460
by control of credit, by the nature of the
recipient regimes, etc.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.360
But both of these efforts it seems bore little
fruit.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.939
What went wrong?

00:08:13.939 --> 00:08:15.400
General Scowcroft, would you start?

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:20.389
General Scowcroft: Well, I think they bore
little fruit because I think they were well

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:28.629
meaning but fundamentally misguided and that
is they operated on the premise, the simple

00:08:28.629 --> 00:08:35.600
and in many ways comforting premise that arms
create wars and that therefore it's intrinsically

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:41.410
an evil and should be engaged in only when
our overriding national interests are involved.

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:44.820
And it seems to me that fundamentally misses
the point.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:50.260
Long before there were complicated weapon
systems, long before there was gunpowder there

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.270
was war.

00:08:52.270 --> 00:09:01.060
Conflict is older than recorded history and
it's deeply rooted in the nature of man.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:08.200
And it seems to me that arms and arms races
really represent the thermometer, not the

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:14.070
disease and that we're not likely to cure
the disease by breaking the thermometer.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:22.120
Now I think certainly a lot of our friends
use arms or want arms for purposes we do not

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.430
consider overriding.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:32.560
Nevertheless, are we really to set ourselves
up as the judge of the fundamental national

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.340
interests of our friends?

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:42.220
And it seems to me that the answer to that
question is fundamentally what was wrong with

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.500
the policy of the previous administration.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:49.660
Senator Biden: If I may, I think I disagree
with the general.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:52.660
At least to some degree.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:10:00.850
I would suggest that the real issue is not
whether or not nations in effect would fight

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.050
anyway and I think the general's correct.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:09.600
I mean, whether or not they had the SAM missiles
provided by the Soviet Union or whether or

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:15.340
not they had sophisticated airplanes supplied
by the United States or France...if they didn't

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:19.690
have those they'd use slingshots or rocks
or bow and arrows or whatever.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:26.150
But I do think US national interest is impacted
upon significantly depending on the degree

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:28.620
to which...of sophistication of the weaponry.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:36.150
For example, it is much less likely that a
conflict that is limited to obsolete tanks

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:44.930
and old M1 riffles or their counterparts or
Remington rifles is going to embroil a whole

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:51.620
region of the world and in fact bring the
United States into a confrontation it would

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:57.320
otherwise like to avoid thereby either bringing
in the Soviets, or the Soviets first making

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:00.340
it an east-west confrontation.

00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:06.420
I think that the extent to which that occurs
at least in some part depends upon the degree

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.691
of sophistication of the weapons.

00:11:08.691 --> 00:11:12.450
The reason why the Congress got in the business
of saying that we wanna have at least a say

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:18.001
in that because it is a component of foreign
policy was because it went from $1.7 billion

00:11:18.001 --> 00:11:22.040
to somewhere in the order of $16 billion in
a matter of 4 years.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:27.110
And it looked as though to some of us that
was becoming almost the sole criteria for

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:30.180
determining what reach we would have abroad.

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:33.950
And I am much more Machiavellian I guess than
my friend, the general.

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:40.660
And in fact I do think if we are transferring
arms we should first and foremost conclude

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:43.980
whether or not it impacts favorably on our
national interest.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.720
If it does and yet it does not impact on their
interest, I would vote to send the arms.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:55.990
If it does not yet it does favorably impact
on them I would be inclined not to send the

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:56.990
arms.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:58.110
That's an oversimplification.

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:02.920
And last point in the regard to transfers,
there are two elements of the transfers.

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:08.620
We compete not only with our Soviet friends...they
are our enemy.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:13.750
They are in fact engaged in confrontation
in various levels and stages throughout the

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:18.700
world and we are engaged with them but we
are also engaged with our allies.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:22.680
And it seems to me that one of the things
we have missed and one of the things as chairing

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:29.110
the European committee for some years now
in the Senate is that we are somewhat timid

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:35.470
about speaking to our allies firmly about
what we are...what the quid pro quo for them

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:38.190
doing such and such is.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:44.670
And so we tend to say...we say we are as helpless
in dealing with French sales which may be

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:51.530
true, British sales or any other ally as we
are with Soviet sales to Third World countries

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:55.850
and I think we should at some point at least
in the Congress address whether or not we

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:59.230
are...have been tough enough where we can
be tough.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:04.320
Representative Aspin: Let me agree with some
of what General Scowcroft said and some of

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:05.620
what Senator Biden said.

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:11.360
I think that it's true that arms do not create
the conflict.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:16.750
The conflict was there before the arms, that
the conflict will go on whether we provide

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:24.150
the arms or not, that...who are we to judge
their national security interests.

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:26.250
They have to look at their own national security
interests.

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:30.950
But we...it's our decision as to whether we
are to sell them a particular weapon or weapon

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:32.210
system.

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:38.520
And I think that given the limited leverage
implied by your statement which I agree with

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:44.550
that we have limited leverage even if we provide
them the arms that we had better make pretty

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:53.130
sure that their national security interests
at least in the use of that weapon is consistent

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:54.130
with ours.

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:55.560
John Charles Daly: Congressman Dickinson?

00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:01.630
Representative Dickinson: Well, I don't know
that I can add much to this particular point

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:12.130
except to underscore the fact that we do sell
arms should be to promote our national interests,

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:14.550
not to help that country necessarily.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:19.090
If by helping that country we promote our
interests, yes.

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:26.920
But then getting back to Les's point, I don't
know that we can properly put ourselves in

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:32.861
the role of judge and say, "Well, we don't
believe that this is in your national interest

00:14:32.861 --> 00:14:38.320
so country of Chile, we won't sell you a sophisticated
airplane."

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:41.510
So they go and buy a Mirage from France.

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:47.730
Or we say to South Africa, "We don't like
your internal politics.

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:53.210
We need you strategically, we need your minerals
but we won't sell you a submarine."

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:54.571
So what do they do?

00:14:54.571 --> 00:14:56.630
They go to France and buy submarines.

00:14:56.630 --> 00:15:03.040
We haven't denied anything except we put a
strain on the relationship and maybe to the

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.170
point of breach at some time.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:13.750
But you see, when you talk about weapon sales
that's just a part of it.

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:21.530
And there is a pending deal now with Saudi
Arabia for about $8.5 billion.

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:26.890
Well, if you break that down only about $2
billion, $2 billion to $3 billion is actually

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:28.490
for weapons.

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:34.450
The rest of it is going into hospitals, it
goes into the infrastructure, it goes to train

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:36.140
people.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:38.010
Their people come over here, we go over there.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:39.010
There's a cultural exchange.

00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:43.730
And so there's a very large ripple effect
than...I just picked Saudi because they have

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:46.760
the largest pending sale now.

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:52.530
But in countries around the world all of these
ripple effects are very important to us.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:57.750
And so we have to look at what is our national
interest and it'd be very difficult...it is

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:00.210
very difficult to say, "Well, we won't sell
it to you."

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:01.990
And then force them to go someplace else.

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:02.990
John Charles Daly: General, you want...

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:03.990
General Scowcroft: Yep.

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:08.120
Well, I think it goes without saying, senator,
that our national interest is what we're concerned

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:10.600
about, not the national interest of the recipient.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:18.180
But presumably our national interest is involved
in making them feel more secure for some purpose

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:23.050
that we have in mind and it's that that I
meant when we say, "You don't really need

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.100
those things.

00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:26.100
We wanna help you.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.510
We're your friend but you don't really need
those weapons."

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:32.770
And it's that that I think gets over to the
point Congressman Dickinson had.

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:34.680
Senator Biden: Two brief points with regard
to that.

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:40.210
First of all, it seems to me that a point
that Congressman Dickinson just made...take

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:42.560
South Africa for an example.

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:48.220
The implication is because South Africa went
to an ally, France, or will go to an ally,

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:53.170
France, to get the same thing we wouldn't
sell them that somehow that doesn't benefit

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:54.170
us particularly.

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:57.560
Let me suggest that that may be of immense
benefit to us.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:03.290
Let's assume that one of the downsides I think
we'll all agree, potential downside...it doesn't

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:09.660
always occur but one of the downsides when
it occurs of an arms transfer to another country

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:17.600
may very well be that we feel we have to because
of security reasons give aid, military aid

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:21.410
but we know we're gonna suffer propaganda
price for having done so.

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:26.320
So I would suggest that in a sophisticated
arms transfer policy...and I realize I'm coming

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:30.850
off as the young prince here, Machiavelli,
but I would suggest that sometimes it makes

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:38.710
sense for us to forego the downside which
is the propaganda loss as a consequence of

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:44.020
a transfer to what we would acknowledge as
a regime that doesn't particularly have values

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:49.880
compatible with what we view ours to be and
yet accomplish the same goal by having an

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:53.850
ally move and fill that vacuum.

00:17:53.850 --> 00:18:00.670
Second point is that with regard to the question
of insecurities of foreign countries I think

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:05.600
one of the problems that we sometimes face,
democratic and republic administrations has

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:10.950
been that we fuel the insecurities of insecure
leaders.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:15.230
We give them a false sense of security by
the transfer of arms.

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:19.370
I can think of no greater case in point than
Iran.

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:22.240
I can think of no instance in recent history.

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:27.750
Quite frankly the history of the United States
of America in transfer of arms where we in

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:32.520
fact kidded ourselves and allowed the shah
to kid himself.

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:38.070
Here we had close to $30 billion worth of
the most sophisticated arms in the world and

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:39.780
not a shot was fired.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:42.160
Not a single shot that I'm aware of.

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:43.710
He was marched out of the country.

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:48.590
His successor was marched out of the country
and all those weapons are either lying dormant

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:54.781
and/or have become...they have been accessed
by the KGB and the Soviet Union and/or are

00:18:54.781 --> 00:18:58.730
being prepared to be used in a way if they
ever get their act together that may be by

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:00.230
inimicable to our interests.

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:03.860
We're now told with regard to Saudi Arabia
the reason why we should sell them certain

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:08.240
weapons including AWACS is because there's
a runway in Iran.

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:12.850
And Iran has those F and...well, I won't go
through all the weaponry but Iran has those

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:17.250
weapons that can take off, be across the Gulf
in a matter of minutes and now we have to

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:23.160
supply a nation with sophisticated weaponry
to shoot down the sophisticated weaponry we

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:28.670
gave an insecure leader who we should've known
we were just feeding his insecurity if we

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:33.560
had been willing to look beyond our own intelligence
apparatus, training of their intelligence

00:19:33.560 --> 00:19:36.100
apparatus through whom we got the advice.

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:38.433
That's an overstatement I'm afraid but that's
a tendency of senators, I guess.

00:19:38.433 --> 00:19:39.480
John Charles Daly: It's very interesting.

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:41.110
General, would you like to take on...

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:42.110
General Scowcroft: Yes.

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:46.670
I think it's a substantial overstatement and
it seems to me that recent events in Iran,

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:51.620
if they've shown anything, it's that the situation
with the shah is far more complicated than

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:53.780
it appeared at the time.

00:19:53.780 --> 00:20:00.360
In 1971 when the British pulled out of the
Gulf we had 3 ways to go.

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:05.990
We could try to keep them there and subsidize
them, we could replace them with our own forces

00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:09.700
or we could try to create a bastion of strength
there.

00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:10.980
We chose the third.

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:11.980
Man: A correct choice in my opinion.

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:17.882
General Scowcroft: Now I suggest that it was
highly successful and that while the shah

00:20:17.882 --> 00:20:20.910
was there the Gulf was stable.

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:24.600
There wasn't a war between Iran and Iraq.

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:25.860
It didn't cost us anything.

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:30.430
As a matter of fact, economically it benefited
us.

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:35.800
I think it's a moot point how much it did
or did not have to do with the shah's fall

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:40.000
but afterwards what have we had to replace
the shah with?

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:46.940
Two carrier task forces, an RDF that we're
trying to fund, billions of dollars that is

00:20:46.940 --> 00:20:51.480
gonna cost the United States to replace what
we had in the shah.

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:52.570
Senator Biden: I don't disagree that we should...

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:56.120
John Charles Daly: May I just throw that RDF being
a rapid deployment force.

00:20:56.120 --> 00:21:02.750
What other hazards of having sophisticated
weaponry of the kind that we did transfer

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:03.750
to the shah...

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:07.960
Senator Biden: May I make...and I think the
fundamental question is...was all of that

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:13.490
weapons that we sold to the shah necessary
to play the role that General Scowcroft is

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:14.990
saying that we wanted the shah to play.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:20.840
I think that the point is correct that we
were looking for a substitute for the Brits,

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:26.730
we were looking for somebody to provide some
stability in the area but does that mean F14s,

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:32.020
DD-963 destroyers...I mean, does the whole
baggage have to come with that?

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:33.190
My answer to that would be no.

00:21:33.190 --> 00:21:34.850
I mean, there's a role to play.

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:39.030
There was an...an arms sales would've been
a part of it.

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:43.080
But all of these extremely sophisticated weapons
I think...

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:49.230
You could argue that it made his military
force less effective than it might've been.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:54.220
It stretched their technology, their technical
people very, very thin.

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:59.250
We never know because of course the shah's
army and air force and navy was never put

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:00.250
to the test.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:04.550
It crumbled from within rather than was defeated
from...it never...so we never know.

00:22:04.550 --> 00:22:09.680
But I think that you don't have to sell them
everything to follow the policy that you're

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:10.680
advocating there.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:13.900
General Scowcroft: I wouldn't disagree with
that and I think reasonable people can disagree

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:16.100
over weapon system by weapon system.

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:20.530
John Charles Daly: When Representative Dickinson
raised very briefly an issue that became a

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:28.780
cause célèbre of the Saudi Arabian
purchase which included the AWACS.

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:33.210
So this is an area in which we are caught
between the nether and the near millstone.

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:39.070
It's almost impossible to come out of it without
making somebody upset at us.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:46.460
Is this an area that is so common and constant
in this field that we really should shrink

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:51.900
from the whole arms transfer business as much
as possible because of the problems they can

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:52.900
raise?

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:57.510
Senator Biden: I think there's one thing that's
part of this is that there's maybe an asymmetry

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:02.590
here that you don't get much from selling
it but you might get a lot...get hurt a lot

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:06.420
if you announce that you're gonna...thinking
about selling it and then deny the sale.

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:08.100
General Scowcroft: I think there's a lot of
truth in there.

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:16.190
Senator Biden: And I think that that's part
of the arms sale problem is this asymmetry

00:23:16.190 --> 00:23:21.940
that it...that by selling it we may get a
little bit of leverage, we may get some goodwill,

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:28.830
we may gain a little bit but the downside
if you don't sell it turns out to be enormous

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:29.830
with these countries.

00:23:29.830 --> 00:23:35.290
I mean, they take it as a personal affront
and a rejection and I think that's what gets

00:23:35.290 --> 00:23:44.780
us into trouble is that we make these deliberations
in public as we...as you do but if the decision

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:47.390
turns out negative it can have severe consequences.

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:50.730
Representative Aspin: I don't disagree with
that but I would like to add one point about

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:51.730
Saudi Arabia.

00:23:51.730 --> 00:23:57.050
I think Saudi Arabia falls into the category
that I arbitrarily said I thought there were

00:23:57.050 --> 00:23:58.050
two categories.

00:23:58.050 --> 00:24:00.280
One where if we don't supply, an ally will
supply.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:04.010
And the other one is if we don't supply, the
Russians are coming and they're gonna move

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:09.820
in and they're gonna become the supplier and
we're gonna have a reverse Ethiopia or Somalia

00:24:09.820 --> 00:24:10.820
or Egypt.

00:24:10.820 --> 00:24:13.591
They're all the places we can know where we've
each been suppliers and one moves out and

00:24:13.591 --> 00:24:15.540
one moves in.

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:22.429
In the case of Saudi Arabia, I think that
there is no more complicated part of the world

00:24:22.429 --> 00:24:23.670
than the Middle East.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:25.970
Not many I think would disagree with that.

00:24:25.970 --> 00:24:36.040
And we have what appear to be, appear I emphasize,
conflicting foreign policy goals and the transfers

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:42.050
of this...of sophisticated weaponry to either
Israel or to any Arab state, in this case

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:48.390
the Saudis, can further complicate that so
that we started off I thought or we...I think

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:55.970
we should start off with saying, "Is our arms
transfers a part of foreign policy?"

00:24:55.970 --> 00:25:02.070
And I think the problem we have in the Middle
East is the determination of our foreign policy.

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:05.510
Now foreign policy can be complicated by an
arms transfer.

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:10.930
And let me just give you one case in point
where I didn't think that sale should go forward.

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:15.690
Forget all the questions about whether or
not Israel was more or less secure, whether...what

00:25:15.690 --> 00:25:19.590
it does, whether it'll work, how much of an
umbrella it presents, etc.

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:25.370
It seems to me that the Saudis have been very
deft at seeing to it that they give little

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:29.140
more than lip service to involvement in any
Mid East war up to now.

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:32.470
They've essentially said to their Arab friends
when they said, "Jump on with us."

00:25:32.470 --> 00:25:34.010
They've said, "But we'll send money.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:35.010
We wish you luck.

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:36.620
We'll pray at the mosque for you."

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:37.770
And so on.

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:42.191
But now it seems to me because the interest
we're looking to protect there is oil to the

00:25:42.191 --> 00:25:45.800
western world and Japan...I mean, I think
we can be as fairly naked.

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:51.990
We're in a situation where I think the sale
of this sophisticated weaponry makes Saudi

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:56.750
Arabia a valuable ally to the rest of the
Arab world if there is confrontation.

00:25:56.750 --> 00:26:03.230
And failure to join the cause and engage that
weaponry if confrontation occurs I think will

00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:08.930
result in what is the most likely thing to
occur and that is internal espionage and sabotage,

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:12.710
those wells being blown up by some of the
two million Palestinians who run them now

00:26:12.710 --> 00:26:18.179
anyway and the end result will be the foreign
policy initiative we were setting out to protect,

00:26:18.179 --> 00:26:23.290
that is free access to that oil, may very
well be hindered instead of helped by the

00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:24.290
transfer.

00:26:24.290 --> 00:26:26.230
John Charles Daly: General Scowcroft?

00:26:26.230 --> 00:26:28.820
General Scowcroft: I wonder if that's really
the case.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:32.640
In the first place the Saudis have given token
help before.

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:37.330
It seems to me in the '67 war they sent a
brigade or a battalion.

00:26:37.330 --> 00:26:38.330
I'm not sure it ever got there.

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:39.330
Representative Aspin: Yeah.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:40.330
It's only been token.

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:41.330
That's the point.

00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:42.330
General Scowcroft: That's right.

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:43.330
They have done things.

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:47.895
And I'm not sure that that would not be the
case in any future...as a matter of fact I'd

00:26:47.895 --> 00:26:53.430
say the reverse would be true that it would
be involved mostly because the Israelis might

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:58.620
feel it was vital to take out that AWACS in
case a war started.

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:00.950
I think that's, you know, that's a possibility.

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:04.160
Senator Biden: General, as a military planner
wouldn't you assume that if you were an Israeli

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:05.160
planner?

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:08.760
Wouldn't you assume if there's a war that
those F15s are not gonna sit on a runway and

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:15.000
those AWACS are not gonna be just focused
on the east and that you better hit first.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:16.000
I mean, you're a general.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:19.170
I mean, wouldn't you sit there and say, "That's
the logical thing to do."

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:23.340
General Scowcroft: I would certainly make
plans for it but the Israelis have not hit

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:27.500
all the Arab targets that they had the capability
of hitting in previous wars.

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:32.750
Senator Biden: But none have had as...the
ones they haven't hit have not had the potential

00:27:32.750 --> 00:27:38.550
capability to wreak havoc on them, that the
weaponry we are now selling will and does.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:39.761
That's the only point I'm...

00:27:39.761 --> 00:27:40.761
Representative Aspin: That's true.

00:27:40.761 --> 00:27:47.000
But, you know, but this may be a case which
in a sensory future argument about the sophistication

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:53.840
being an evil, in fact it may be a benefit
here because the equipment is so sophisticated.

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:58.920
And that may be the case in a number of cases,
that it cannot usefully be operated without

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:06.160
American help which gives us additional leverage
to ensure, unlike M1 rifles and grenades and

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:10.970
so on that they don't need any help for, there's
this additional leverage in the way that those

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:12.260
weapons will be used.

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:21.370
Representative Dickinson: It seems to me that
we completely ignore and look aside at the

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:28.809
real issue but if we talk about AWACS and
is...how is it in our best interest and what

00:28:28.809 --> 00:28:31.130
action should we take?

00:28:31.130 --> 00:28:42.160
We are almost governed, it's too strong a
word, but we...the Israeli government and

00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:49.660
the supporters of Israel have an inordinate
impact on our Congress and that's really what...we're

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:54.660
not...the president has said it's in the interest
of the United States, Secretary Haig has said

00:28:54.660 --> 00:28:55.800
it's in the interest.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:29:01.250
If you look to see the history of Saudi in
the past few years and the friendship they

00:29:01.250 --> 00:29:08.049
have offered us...and I'm talking about OPEC
now, OPEC was just trying to raise their price

00:29:08.049 --> 00:29:10.580
from $32 to $36 a barrel.

00:29:10.580 --> 00:29:13.080
It was the Saudis that kept them from doing
that.

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:15.700
They have done that on at least three different
occasions.

00:29:15.700 --> 00:29:19.620
It was the Saudis that kept the Jordanians
and others that wanted OPEC to go off the

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:24.100
dollar standard onto another monetary exchange.

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:32.760
So there have been any number of instances
where we've been the beneficiary of the friendship

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:34.500
of the Saudis.

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:41.130
And to...for us to say that it's not in our
interest to continue the friendship and to

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:45.070
give them a defensive capability and that's
what we're talking about here and not the

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:52.280
F15s but in AWACS I think it would be particularly
shortsighted and I would certainly support

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:53.280
it.

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:55.080
Representative Aspin: You can't let it go
by.

00:29:55.080 --> 00:30:00.730
I think that it's important that...to...when
you look at what the Saudis have done with

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:06.140
oil prices, what they've done in the economic
sphere it's very clear that they have not

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:10.160
done anything that is not out of line with
their own self-interest.

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:11.160
There's a...

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:12.160
Representative Dickinson: Wait for just a
moment.

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.160
What about keeping the prices down?

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:14.160
You think that was...

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:15.160
Representative Aspin: Absolutely.

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:16.160
Absolutely.

00:30:16.160 --> 00:30:17.160
Absolutely.

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:18.290
Representative Dickinson: But all of the other
nations wanted to raise it in OPEC.

00:30:18.290 --> 00:30:19.290
Representative Aspin: That's right.

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:24.751
But they perceived it in their own self-interest
to keep the oil prices down because if the

00:30:24.751 --> 00:30:32.970
prices go up the substitution for oil in the
industrial west becomes quicker, their resources

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:38.550
of oil in their...in the ground becomes obsolete
quicker.

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:43.260
But I don't think you can look at anything
in the oil and the economic sphere and make

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:46.410
a case that Saudi Arabia is doing us any favors.

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:50.650
I mean, they may be doing us some favors as
a byproduct of doing themselves some favors.

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:54.660
What would be really impressive to me would
be if they would throw their weight in behind

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:56.110
the Camp David Accords.

00:30:56.110 --> 00:30:58.750
Now that would be impressive.

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:03.620
And in that case that might change my view
about things like the sale of the F15s and

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:04.620
the AWACS.

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:06.400
General Scowcroft: And if I could just add
one point.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:11.230
It seems to me we have another interest in
the AWACS in Saudi Arabia aside from what

00:31:11.230 --> 00:31:16.630
you've discussed and that is a US national
interest to protect our own forces over there,

00:31:16.630 --> 00:31:17.630
you know.

00:31:17.630 --> 00:31:24.050
We used to have long-range radar in Iran that
could monitor the Soviet Union and any Soviet

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:25.059
attack.

00:31:25.059 --> 00:31:28.090
Our battle fleets there are potentially very
vulnerable.

00:31:28.090 --> 00:31:29.370
Man: That's right.

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:34.780
General Scowcroft: To Soviet air attack and
it seems to me that the AWACS serves a very

00:31:34.780 --> 00:31:39.630
important role and is right now in giving
us early warning of a possible Soviet attack.

00:31:39.630 --> 00:31:41.740
Senator Biden: But general you know better
than I do.

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:45.820
I just spent two days out at our...a day out
at Tinker Air Force Base and spent all kinds

00:31:45.820 --> 00:31:47.870
of time in the Intelligence Committee.

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:54.030
The AWACS flown within the boundaries of Saudi
Arabia does not give us any warning that has

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:57.559
anything comparable to that which in fact
existed in Iran.

00:31:57.559 --> 00:32:02.750
We have a very limited ability as broad as
it is to be able to detect whether or not

00:32:02.750 --> 00:32:08.920
there is a Soviet move and it is really...it's
not even remotely comparable to the Iranian position.

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:10.220
General Scowcroft: Absolutely.

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:14.140
But it is a great deal better than the self-protection
of the carrier.

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:15.140
Senator Biden: Right.

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:16.140
And we have it now.

00:32:16.140 --> 00:32:17.140
We have four AWACS there.

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:18.669
They are flying now.

00:32:18.669 --> 00:32:20.059
They're American cruised.

00:32:20.059 --> 00:32:21.780
They're controlled by America.

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:22.780
And...

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:25.760
General Scowcroft: And they're there at Saudi's
sufferance.

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:27.880
Senator Biden: And Saudi needs them as much
as we do.

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:28.880
Yeah.

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:34.830
John Charles Daly: Well, I think we've gone into
the...I'd like to come back to the fact that

00:32:34.830 --> 00:32:43.890
since '72 the Congress has with some assertiveness
moved to try to reduce this issue of arms

00:32:43.890 --> 00:32:44.890
transfers.

00:32:44.890 --> 00:32:51.040
And I wonder if the efforts of past administrations
and the Congress have not produced a labyrinth

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:54.510
and maze of administrative processes.

00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:59.340
Some elements of this program could go through
state, the Defense Departments, the Joint

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:06.720
Chiefs of Staff, the individual services,
the Treasury Department, the Agency of International

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:11.130
Development, the Central Intelligence Agency,
the Office of Management and Budget and we

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:18.610
constructed a system which is just incapable
of flexible response to volatile and ever-changing

00:33:18.610 --> 00:33:22.360
worlds.

00:33:22.360 --> 00:33:23.360
Congress bill.

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:27.280
A lot of these...and initially what...you...this...you're...you
were in the Congress actually through all

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:31.280
this from '72 to '77 when all that legislation
was going through.

00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:32.280
It doesn't work.

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:33.980
I think we all seem to agree that nothing's
worked.

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:38.330
Representative Aspin: It doesn't work in the
sense that arms sales have continued to increase.

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:43.690
What we have done is as you say set up a rather
elaborate procedure in which the Congress

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:49.440
must approve and you have got various players
in the field.

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:55.710
But once you set up a procedure like that
it's hard to be sure that the thing wouldn't

00:33:55.710 --> 00:33:59.120
have...would've gone exactly the same without
that procedure.

00:33:59.120 --> 00:34:04.799
Yes, Congress has never voted down an arms
sales that has been proposed.

00:34:04.799 --> 00:34:09.839
Has an administration ever been dissuaded
from proposing an arms sale because of this

00:34:09.839 --> 00:34:14.149
procedure and the fear that Congress might
turn it down?

00:34:14.149 --> 00:34:21.049
You know, I think it's very hard to prove
conclusively that this has been a total failure.

00:34:21.049 --> 00:34:24.429
Senator Biden: Could I suggest something,
Les, that I...it seems to me that there are

00:34:24.429 --> 00:34:31.309
two reasons for the labyrinth, as you refer
to it, that the Congress set up.

00:34:31.309 --> 00:34:37.609
One has been I think noticeably and unsuccessful
and one has been very successful.

00:34:37.609 --> 00:34:42.179
The first reason was that we hoped, everyone
hoped I think that...who has participated

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:49.109
in it that it would in fact cut down in terms
of the gross quantity of weaponry and dollars

00:34:49.109 --> 00:34:50.940
represented in that weaponry that was transferred.

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:57.249
To that extent on a chart that you would draw
and the dollars, sort of weapon value transferred

00:34:57.249 --> 00:34:59.130
it hasn't been very successful.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:03.960
But a second and I believe more important
reason why and I was there from '72 on when

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:08.660
we were doing this also in the Senate was
the foreign policy component of it because

00:35:08.660 --> 00:35:12.260
at the outset of this program we all said,
the one thing we all agree on, it is a tool

00:35:12.260 --> 00:35:13.859
of foreign policy.

00:35:13.859 --> 00:35:20.749
And what happened was we had presidents, Democrats
and Republicans over the previous 15 to...probably

00:35:20.749 --> 00:35:28.869
over our history who were in fact treating
what was viewed as an economic decision independent

00:35:28.869 --> 00:35:34.349
of the foreign policy consideration that the
Congress said we now wanna be part of.

00:35:34.349 --> 00:35:39.880
And to that extent I think it's successful
and has been successful in the...you may argue

00:35:39.880 --> 00:35:43.759
Congress wasn't right but it's been successful
and accomplished what Congress set out to

00:35:43.759 --> 00:35:45.440
do and that is be a part of the game.

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:46.989
John Charles Daly: General Scowcroft?

00:35:46.989 --> 00:35:51.619
General Scowcroft: I think Congress ought
to be involved in these kinds of decisions.

00:35:51.619 --> 00:35:54.230
They are fundamentally foreign policy decisions.

00:35:54.230 --> 00:35:58.410
I think that Congress really, and this is
very difficult to do, ought to be involved

00:35:58.410 --> 00:35:59.720
at the policy stage.

00:35:59.720 --> 00:36:05.760
The problem I have with the present procedures
is it's too late when you all get it.

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:13.739
And you cannot reject a proposal of an administration
without doing grievous foreign policy harm

00:36:13.739 --> 00:36:14.739
at that point.

00:36:14.739 --> 00:36:15.739
Senator Biden: I couldn't agree with you more.

00:36:15.739 --> 00:36:16.739
General Scowcroft: That's the problem.

00:36:16.739 --> 00:36:19.079
John Charles Daly: I think we've covered a lot of
ground and perhaps raised a good number of

00:36:19.079 --> 00:36:21.640
questions among our friends in the audience.

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:23.750
So it's time for the question and answer session.

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:25.530
May I have the first question please?

00:36:25.530 --> 00:36:28.029
Harry: I'm Harry Frack from the Olen Corporation.

00:36:28.029 --> 00:36:36.729
I noticed all kinds of concerns and effects
of arms transfers but did not find anyone

00:36:36.729 --> 00:36:44.119
saying anything about the domestic effects
both economic and in our military preparedness.

00:36:44.119 --> 00:36:49.570
About 50% of our companies have been manufacturing
goods for foreign sales which is about the

00:36:49.570 --> 00:36:53.299
only thing that's kept our own industry alive
in the defense products area.

00:36:53.299 --> 00:36:54.700
John Charles Daly: All right.

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:55.900
Who'd like to start there?

00:36:55.900 --> 00:36:58.499
Representative Dickinson: Well, I'll take
it,

00:36:58.499 --> 00:37:00.660
to start with.

00:37:00.660 --> 00:37:02.400
And you're exactly correct.

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:09.130
Our arms sales has a very wide impact economically
in this country.

00:37:09.130 --> 00:37:16.119
As a matter of fact, I was reading some...I
don't know how authoritative it is but some

00:37:16.119 --> 00:37:23.050
50,000 families are dependent on sales to
Saudi Arabia alone in this proposed package.

00:37:23.050 --> 00:37:27.009
I don't know who put out that propaganda but
it is very, very wide.

00:37:27.009 --> 00:37:34.820
And I was just in a defense plant on the West
Coast last week and they were making armored

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:40.000
personnel vehicles and they don't have any
sales to this country now.

00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:44.630
They are going to Spain, they go into Israel,
they're going to any number of other countries

00:37:44.630 --> 00:37:47.849
but that's what's keeping them going as you
said.

00:37:47.849 --> 00:37:55.320
So it is a very broad...if we lost all our
foreign military sales right now the unemployment

00:37:55.320 --> 00:37:57.380
rate would go up substantially in this country.

00:37:57.380 --> 00:37:58.380
John Charles Daly: General Scowcroft?

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:02.640
General Scowcroft: Well, I don't think there's
any question that it is helpful to American

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:04.849
industry and also to our own armed forces.

00:38:04.849 --> 00:38:08.509
It cuts substantially the unit cost of some
of our equipment.

00:38:08.509 --> 00:38:15.060
But I'll be something of an iconoclast, I
do not think that our arms sale policy ought

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:18.299
to be dictated by our domestic economic needs.

00:38:18.299 --> 00:38:22.839
I think that'll be dictated by our national
interest and solely on that basis.

00:38:22.839 --> 00:38:29.599
Senator Biden: Also one other point, a minor
point in this thing and that is that yes,

00:38:29.599 --> 00:38:35.779
there are cases where we have sold arms abroad
where there was no demand in the American

00:38:35.779 --> 00:38:39.050
armed forces for that equipment and so it
did keep people at work.

00:38:39.050 --> 00:38:44.720
There are also cases and probably more now
if we're into a buildup phase in the US military

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:50.640
where you've got competing claims where the
US military also wants the equipment and the

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:54.920
arms sales are competing and you get the question
who gets it first.

00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:56.489
Representative Dickinson: Like the F16.

00:38:56.489 --> 00:38:58.079
Senator Biden: Yeah, yeah.

00:38:58.079 --> 00:38:59.950
I can think of a few.

00:38:59.950 --> 00:39:01.410
So it cuts both ways in that regard now.

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:02.410
General Scowcroft: That's true.

00:39:02.410 --> 00:39:07.339
Senator Biden: I would like to add one point,
one additional point and I think the gentleman's...the

00:39:07.339 --> 00:39:09.369
questioner's point is well taken.

00:39:09.369 --> 00:39:14.749
It does positively impact upon our balance
of trade situation.

00:39:14.749 --> 00:39:19.079
But it is one of the potential upsides to
arms sales.

00:39:19.079 --> 00:39:25.749
But to reiterate General Scowcroft's point
if in fact we have a circumstance where it

00:39:25.749 --> 00:39:33.609
becomes the motivating force behind the sale,
not only have we sometimes not had in production

00:39:33.609 --> 00:39:39.089
military equipment that our military no longer
needed, what we've tended to do is our arms

00:39:39.089 --> 00:39:46.140
merchants have decided to go sell what was
also not needed by the third country, that

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:48.019
particular weapon system.

00:39:48.019 --> 00:39:49.019
That has occurred too.

00:39:49.019 --> 00:39:50.019
So it's a double edged sword.

00:39:50.019 --> 00:39:51.260
General Scowcroft: Yeah, yeah.

00:39:51.260 --> 00:39:52.270
John Charles Daly: All right.

00:39:52.270 --> 00:39:53.270
Next question please.

00:39:53.270 --> 00:39:56.700
Bill: Yes, my name is Bill Wickersham with
the World Federalist Association and I would

00:39:56.700 --> 00:40:00.499
direct this to Congressman Aspin.

00:40:00.499 --> 00:40:05.079
Most of the panels indicated that they think
the United States should act in its own self-interest

00:40:05.079 --> 00:40:08.150
which probably very few people would disagree.

00:40:08.150 --> 00:40:16.170
The question is if 159 or so other nations
around the world act in the same way and if

00:40:16.170 --> 00:40:21.999
in fact the weaponry becomes more sophisticated
and it becomes dispersed throughout the world

00:40:21.999 --> 00:40:27.809
and if in fact as several studies including
some United States government studies suggest

00:40:27.809 --> 00:40:33.999
there will be a nuclear capability within
some 29 or 30 nations by let's say year 2000

00:40:33.999 --> 00:40:40.219
for sure, how do we contain this so it's not
combustible and we...the whole thing explodes.

00:40:40.219 --> 00:40:43.170
Representative Aspin: That is a very good
question.

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:48.710
I think that the phenomenon that you're talking
about is a very serious one and a very real

00:40:48.710 --> 00:40:49.710
one.

00:40:49.710 --> 00:40:55.700
If you look at the amount of money spent on
arms in the world and just plot that over

00:40:55.700 --> 00:40:58.799
a number of years, it's really reaching staggering
proportions.

00:40:58.799 --> 00:41:06.029
I am not sure that you can stem that tide
with an arms sales policy alone.

00:41:06.029 --> 00:41:11.229
If you had an across the board policy which
I think is unwise to take an extreme position

00:41:11.229 --> 00:41:23.260
of not selling anything to anybody no matter
what I think you would be making a not terribly

00:41:23.260 --> 00:41:25.450
significant dent on that race.

00:41:25.450 --> 00:41:32.829
I think those...the factors that go into arms
races are factors that have to do with...in

00:41:32.829 --> 00:41:39.320
national states, there are rivalries with
their neighbors, competing ideologies, competing

00:41:39.320 --> 00:41:47.770
religions, all of these things and the impact
of arms sales on that is a factor but a relatively

00:41:47.770 --> 00:41:54.469
minor one which...and I think that the problem
is that people tend to look at arms sales

00:41:54.469 --> 00:41:59.180
as a good tool to stem that big race.

00:41:59.180 --> 00:42:02.819
And my problem is I don't think it will work.

00:42:02.819 --> 00:42:07.849
And you're putting an awful lot of weight
on a very think reed to try and accomplish

00:42:07.849 --> 00:42:09.960
it through arms sales.

00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:11.499
Through eliminating arms sales.

00:42:11.499 --> 00:42:12.499
Sorry.

00:42:12.499 --> 00:42:13.549
General Scowcroft: If I could just give one
example.

00:42:13.549 --> 00:42:17.540
There's one case where we really tried to
do this and that was Latin America in the

00:42:17.540 --> 00:42:18.540
1960s.

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:20.089
The Alliance for progress.

00:42:20.089 --> 00:42:26.140
We made a policy decision that we were not
gonna sell any arm...any sophisticated arms.

00:42:26.140 --> 00:42:29.869
We were not gonna upgrade any of the equipment
that Latin American countries had.

00:42:29.869 --> 00:42:33.299
It was a relatively stable period in Latin
America.

00:42:33.299 --> 00:42:38.309
The competition between and among the countries
was at a low level.

00:42:38.309 --> 00:42:42.910
Most of their equipment, especially their
airplanes, tanks and so on were obsolete.

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:47.940
We stuck to that during almost all of the
'60s.

00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:53.799
The result was that they bought arms from
the British, bought arms from the French and

00:42:53.799 --> 00:42:55.619
they bought arms from the Russians.

00:42:55.619 --> 00:43:02.809
And now maybe in an ideal world we could get
together with our allies and even with the

00:43:02.809 --> 00:43:07.819
Russians which the Carter administration tried
to put an end to that sort of thing.

00:43:07.819 --> 00:43:11.849
But as a practical matter we don't have very
much leverage.

00:43:11.849 --> 00:43:16.359
Senator Biden: May I...Mister Wickersham,
you...implicit in your statement at least

00:43:16.359 --> 00:43:23.390
as I heard it was that there's an inconsistency
between a transfer of an arms...or between...potentially

00:43:23.390 --> 00:43:29.270
between US self-interest and world harmony.

00:43:29.270 --> 00:43:34.039
And I would suggest that if that is part of
your...implicit in your statement that that

00:43:34.039 --> 00:43:35.039
is not correct.

00:43:35.039 --> 00:43:39.680
I think we're at a stage where one of my colleagues,
southern colleagues in the Senate says, "It's

00:43:39.680 --> 00:43:42.869
wonderful when conscience and convenience
cross paths."

00:43:42.869 --> 00:43:47.469
And I think in arms control policy we have
the opportunity to have both conscience and

00:43:47.469 --> 00:43:48.859
convenience cross paths.

00:43:48.859 --> 00:43:51.519
For example, you mentioned nuclear capability.

00:43:51.519 --> 00:43:57.690
That is the overwhelming concern for the entire
world it seems to me.

00:43:57.690 --> 00:44:04.219
And there are instances where arms transfer
can diminish the prospect of the feeling of

00:44:04.219 --> 00:44:11.339
urgency develop...to develop and/or steal,
and/or whatever a nuclear capability and conversely

00:44:11.339 --> 00:44:17.099
there are cases which I believe is a situation
potentially in Pakistan where massive arms

00:44:17.099 --> 00:44:25.410
transfers can propel the competing countries
in the region to move toward the...a greater

00:44:25.410 --> 00:44:26.789
reliance on nuclear.

00:44:26.789 --> 00:44:32.761
So it can work to the benefit on occasion
and...but that's what I meant by I think we

00:44:32.761 --> 00:44:34.119
should do it case by case.

00:44:34.119 --> 00:44:35.799
John Charles Daly: Next question please.

00:44:35.799 --> 00:44:37.859
Richard: My name is Richard Daryl.

00:44:37.859 --> 00:44:42.489
I'm currently an international affairs fellow
with the Council on Foreign Relations in New

00:44:42.489 --> 00:44:43.489
York.

00:44:43.489 --> 00:44:45.299
I actually have two questions.

00:44:45.299 --> 00:44:46.319
They're interrelated.

00:44:46.319 --> 00:44:53.039
The first is if memory serves correct the
biggest market, the biggest business market

00:44:53.039 --> 00:44:58.210
that the United States has in the world for
its arms sales policies is in fact our allied

00:44:58.210 --> 00:45:01.420
market, and principally our NATO allied market.

00:45:01.420 --> 00:45:03.720
That's where most of the sales go on.

00:45:03.720 --> 00:45:10.529
Yet on the other hand as I understand it NATO
has various policies itself to which we subscribe.

00:45:10.529 --> 00:45:15.289
Policies like rationalization, standardization,
interoperability.

00:45:15.289 --> 00:45:19.720
One implication of which is that the allies
should be able to sell us more.

00:45:19.720 --> 00:45:25.259
They have learned the domestic effects and
the business benefits of arms sales themselves

00:45:25.259 --> 00:45:33.519
in the inter-allied arena and they like to
oftentimes get in on the act.

00:45:33.519 --> 00:45:38.950
That creates however a certain tension between
a US policy which is promoting sales to the

00:45:38.950 --> 00:45:43.470
alliance and an allied policy which wants
reciprocity.

00:45:43.470 --> 00:45:49.299
And my first question then is how do we resolve
this tension and is there room, excuse me,

00:45:49.299 --> 00:45:51.310
for a new policy in this general area?

00:45:51.310 --> 00:45:56.989
My second question arises from something I
think Senator Biden said earlier and that

00:45:56.989 --> 00:46:04.160
was a suggestion that sometimes allied arms
sales abroad are not necessarily detrimental

00:46:04.160 --> 00:46:08.099
to us and they might sometimes be encouraged.

00:46:08.099 --> 00:46:14.369
I wondered when you made that remark whether
or not this ally...in general allied sales

00:46:14.369 --> 00:46:20.109
abroad don't serve as a kind of safety valve
in some ways for the tension in NATO.

00:46:20.109 --> 00:46:22.789
The tension I described earlier.

00:46:22.789 --> 00:46:27.680
And I'm wondering if we have a US policy that
on the one hand either tries to control those

00:46:27.680 --> 00:46:32.880
sales more tightly as some have subscribed
to the...excuse me.

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:38.970
Ascribed to the previous administration or
a policy that tries to promote American arms

00:46:38.970 --> 00:46:44.529
sales abroad more vociferously particularly
if you use an anti-allied argument, "If they

00:46:44.529 --> 00:46:46.109
don't sell it, we will.

00:46:46.109 --> 00:46:48.290
If we don't sell it, they will."

00:46:48.290 --> 00:46:49.290
in the process.

00:46:49.290 --> 00:46:57.609
I wonder if in this respect we don't run the
risk of cutting down or shortchanging the

00:46:57.609 --> 00:47:03.119
safety valve aspects of things and in fact
perhaps endangering the larger NATO allied

00:47:03.119 --> 00:47:08.930
market which is where the real money if you
will often comes into play.

00:47:08.930 --> 00:47:09.930
John Charles Daly: Senator Biden?

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:11.890
Senator Biden: I think you're absolutely correct.

00:47:11.890 --> 00:47:15.460
You pose the problem very...in a very articulate
manner.

00:47:15.460 --> 00:47:18.490
I think we get back to the fundamental question.

00:47:18.490 --> 00:47:22.480
What is our foreign policy and what should
NATO be, how should it be made up and how

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:23.750
effective is it.

00:47:23.750 --> 00:47:27.450
And part of the question of whether or not
it's effective relates to the interoperability,

00:47:27.450 --> 00:47:29.089
the standardization.

00:47:29.089 --> 00:47:31.270
Standardization is not merely motivated.

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:36.029
It is not primarily motivated by the requirement
of the French to get in on the deal.

00:47:36.029 --> 00:47:40.749
It's motivated by the question that generals
know better than senators and that is how

00:47:40.749 --> 00:47:47.550
do you have a unified battle plan without
having radios that can receive one another's

00:47:47.550 --> 00:47:53.069
messages without having spare parts that can
be interchanged without having tanks and planes

00:47:53.069 --> 00:47:59.029
and guns, etc., that are...can be used under
a unified command somewhat like a unified

00:47:59.029 --> 00:48:00.029
army.

00:48:00.029 --> 00:48:02.430
And that is the motivating factor.

00:48:02.430 --> 00:48:07.469
Now once we reach that decision I think the
allies have a right to say, "Hey, wait a minute.

00:48:07.469 --> 00:48:09.670
Now you ain't getting it all.

00:48:09.670 --> 00:48:11.420
We want a piece of that."

00:48:11.420 --> 00:48:15.160
And I think that we have to be willing to
make that decision.

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:23.499
There is a great deal of pressure from our
domestic arms manufacturers to say no, that

00:48:23.499 --> 00:48:28.249
we don't want you "giving away any of our
piece of the market".

00:48:28.249 --> 00:48:35.400
I think just as we've done in many other areas
of foreign relations we have to have the courage

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:41.720
and the...or the foresight to say we're going
to figure out what component, what portion

00:48:41.720 --> 00:48:45.369
you're going to make up, United States, what
we're willing...what piece we're willing to

00:48:45.369 --> 00:48:46.369
take.

00:48:46.369 --> 00:48:52.219
Absent that, there is a great risk being run
in cutting off the safety valve.

00:48:52.219 --> 00:48:57.190
You are absolutely correct because that's
part of the plan.

00:48:57.190 --> 00:49:02.539
I believe because we haven't been willing
to follow through on what is clearly in our

00:49:02.539 --> 00:49:07.440
strategic and conventional interest to do,
that is standardize, and the reason we haven't

00:49:07.440 --> 00:49:13.279
been able to do it is in large part was because
of the economic decisions involved in it.

00:49:13.279 --> 00:49:19.829
Because of that we have propelled to a greater
degree our allies in the markets that compete

00:49:19.829 --> 00:49:27.400
with us in the Third World and many instances
force us to make decisions that are marginally

00:49:27.400 --> 00:49:31.800
less detrimental than if we didn't move into
the area.

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:32.979
I think you state it very well.

00:49:32.979 --> 00:49:33.979
John Charles Daly: General Scowcroft?

00:49:33.979 --> 00:49:37.190
General Scowcroft: I agree with the Senator.

00:49:37.190 --> 00:49:41.849
I think what we need to work on is the NATO
part rather than the safety valve part because...

00:49:41.849 --> 00:49:42.849
Senator Biden: I agree.

00:49:42.849 --> 00:49:47.650
General Scowcroft: You know, NATO spends more
than the Warsaw Pact does on arms and yet,

00:49:47.650 --> 00:49:50.089
you know, we're woefully behind in preparedness.

00:49:50.089 --> 00:49:54.599
One of the reasons is that each country goes
out and builds its own little system.

00:49:54.599 --> 00:49:57.170
They don't work together, they're not standardized
and so on.

00:49:57.170 --> 00:49:58.960
We're one of the biggest culprits.

00:49:58.960 --> 00:50:01.770
Not only for economic reason but military.

00:50:01.770 --> 00:50:09.260
Our own military tend to think that unless
it's our equipment it's not good enough and

00:50:09.260 --> 00:50:14.920
we have worldwide concerns not just in NATO
and therefore we have to have our own unique

00:50:14.920 --> 00:50:15.920
equipment.

00:50:15.920 --> 00:50:17.769
I think we need to do far more.

00:50:17.769 --> 00:50:19.960
And we need to take the lead.

00:50:19.960 --> 00:50:20.990
John Charles Daly: Representative Dickinson?

00:50:20.990 --> 00:50:23.940
Representative Dickinson: I don't disagree
with what has been said.

00:50:23.940 --> 00:50:28.029
I thought maybe I would...it would perhaps
be helpful to put it in a little different

00:50:28.029 --> 00:50:29.029
context.

00:50:29.029 --> 00:50:36.289
For the past few years I've been a delegate
to the North Atlantic Alliance or Assembly

00:50:36.289 --> 00:50:38.930
and this is...the question comes up.

00:50:38.930 --> 00:50:42.989
There is an economic section, a political
section and a military section and I'm on

00:50:42.989 --> 00:50:44.740
the military section.

00:50:44.740 --> 00:50:47.309
And this question comes up every time we meet.

00:50:47.309 --> 00:50:50.709
Well, if we met six times a year that's how
many times it would come up.

00:50:50.709 --> 00:50:53.420
It's a two-way street, RSI.

00:50:53.420 --> 00:50:59.890
And one of the things that causes the breakdown
is that our NATO allies say, "Well, look.

00:50:59.890 --> 00:51:04.749
We're buying more weapons from you than you're
buying from us and so it's not fair."

00:51:04.749 --> 00:51:08.029
And the American reply is, "Well, look.

00:51:08.029 --> 00:51:12.630
You're not counting the cost of us stationing
our troops in Europe and if you put in the

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:16.569
cost of what we're spending to station our
troops in Europe to defend you we're spending

00:51:16.569 --> 00:51:17.650
more than you're spending."

00:51:17.650 --> 00:51:20.490
So it's a...it goes round and round.

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:27.130
But I think in the final analysis of your
major systems that I think the answer is going

00:51:27.130 --> 00:51:30.589
to have to be what we did with the F16.

00:51:30.589 --> 00:51:36.259
That is you're gonna break down and everybody
that wants to get in on the act, all the counties,

00:51:36.259 --> 00:51:40.910
they make components and General Dynamics
is making a part here and the Belgians are

00:51:40.910 --> 00:51:45.900
making a part there and this seems to be working
pretty well and rather than saying, "Well,

00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:49.589
you...we'll build this airplane and you build
that tank and we'll swap out."

00:51:49.589 --> 00:51:54.780
So I think that now the road we're gonna find
is probably the most practical way to build.

00:51:54.780 --> 00:51:57.309
John Charles Daly: All right.

00:51:57.309 --> 00:51:59.500
Next question please.

00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:00.500
Major Hudson: I'm Major Bill Hudson.

00:52:00.500 --> 00:52:04.890
I'm a US Air Force Research Associate at Vance
International Studies Institute.

00:52:04.890 --> 00:52:09.180
Congressman Aspin indicated earlier he felt
that arms sales or arms transfers actually

00:52:09.180 --> 00:52:12.059
had perhaps very little leverage.

00:52:12.059 --> 00:52:14.989
This seems to be the substance of what the
panel discussion is about.

00:52:14.989 --> 00:52:21.089
I wonder if you could all reply concretely
to the fact or to the opinion, do you think

00:52:21.089 --> 00:52:26.619
that arms transfers compare favorable to other
foreign policy tools, diplomatic or economic

00:52:26.619 --> 00:52:32.390
in achieving concrete results such as basing
rights or more generally leverage our influence.

00:52:32.390 --> 00:52:33.890
John Charles Daly: You wanna start, congressman?

00:52:33.890 --> 00:52:38.569
Representative Aspin: I think that what I
meant was that I think leverage is very often

00:52:38.569 --> 00:52:46.920
overstated and it's easy to anticipate a lot
of leverage when in fact the leverage may

00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:48.609
not be very great.

00:52:48.609 --> 00:52:51.239
And I can think of some examples.

00:52:51.239 --> 00:52:57.339
One of the arguments is that arms sales give
us some control over the use of those...of

00:52:57.339 --> 00:53:02.130
that weapons because if you keep control over
the ammunition and the spare parts you're

00:53:02.130 --> 00:53:09.559
then gonna have some...the country a little
bit on a leash as to whether the stuff gets

00:53:09.559 --> 00:53:12.140
used or not.

00:53:12.140 --> 00:53:17.630
But there are military engagements in which
you don't use up a lot of ammunition and spare

00:53:17.630 --> 00:53:19.890
parts.

00:53:19.890 --> 00:53:22.430
Israel bombed the Iraqi reactor.

00:53:22.430 --> 00:53:29.559
One swift blow and you may have all the control
over ammunition and spare parts and it doesn't

00:53:29.559 --> 00:53:31.569
give you much leverage.

00:53:31.569 --> 00:53:34.400
Also countries have a way of building up enough
consumables.

00:53:34.400 --> 00:53:40.779
I think the shah of Iran set up a particular
policy of trying to actually build up enough

00:53:40.779 --> 00:53:46.349
consumables of ammunition and spare parts
for the very purpose of shaking loose and

00:53:46.349 --> 00:53:48.540
getting a little bit more flexibility.

00:53:48.540 --> 00:53:55.430
I mean, in the old days back in the '50s we
seemed to have a lot of leverage but back

00:53:55.430 --> 00:53:58.130
in the '50s there was more than arms sales.

00:53:58.130 --> 00:54:04.999
We were also giving economic grants and all
kinds of other economic aid and the Russians

00:54:04.999 --> 00:54:10.109
were nowhere near as omnipresent as they are
now.

00:54:10.109 --> 00:54:13.759
And so we had a great deal of influence but
I think people look back on that and think

00:54:13.759 --> 00:54:20.009
that the arms sales gave us leverage when
in fact it was a whole constellation of aid

00:54:20.009 --> 00:54:26.130
and assistance and underwriting them financially
which gave us the leverage.

00:54:26.130 --> 00:54:30.450
Leverage is a point but I think it's very
often overstated in the arguments when it

00:54:30.450 --> 00:54:32.180
comes down to a particular arms sale.

00:54:32.180 --> 00:54:33.760
John Charles Daly: Anybody wanna add anything to that?

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:38.500
Senator Biden: Slightly more than Food Through
Peace, slightly less than technology.

00:54:38.500 --> 00:54:44.920
General Scowcroft: I think the question...the
issue is more complicated than influence.

00:54:44.920 --> 00:54:50.400
If by influence you mean we're buying influence
by selling arms and that therefore they'll

00:54:50.400 --> 00:54:57.059
do what we want, I think the answer is clearly
no but it's a much more subtle kind of an

00:54:57.059 --> 00:54:58.059
influence.

00:54:58.059 --> 00:55:01.940
Theoretically it helps these people with their
own purposes.

00:55:01.940 --> 00:55:06.719
It gives them a certain amount of self-confidence.

00:55:06.719 --> 00:55:09.989
In some cases, it's vital to preserving the
military balance.

00:55:09.989 --> 00:55:12.349
You take countries like Korea for example.

00:55:12.349 --> 00:55:18.289
Were we to shut Korea off, you know, the results
could clearly be disaster.

00:55:18.289 --> 00:55:22.729
North Korea gives every indication that if
they thought they could succeed they would

00:55:22.729 --> 00:55:23.729
attack.

00:55:23.729 --> 00:55:28.400
Turkey is another case where a clear military
necessity, others are political.

00:55:28.400 --> 00:55:34.539
But I think you can't just look at cause and
effect.

00:55:34.539 --> 00:55:40.349
I think it's much more subtle and as Congressman
Aspin said earlier in many cases the influence

00:55:40.349 --> 00:55:46.660
is purely negative and that is if you don't
do it you really disrupt your foreign policy.

00:55:46.660 --> 00:55:50.609
Representative Dickinson: Let me give one
small example of what the general's saying.

00:55:50.609 --> 00:55:57.280
I think in the School of the Americas which
is run in Panama to train military officers

00:55:57.280 --> 00:56:02.930
from Latin American countries and in this
country our National War College, Air War

00:56:02.930 --> 00:56:06.400
College, all the services have them, we bring in
foreign students and they're the cream of

00:56:06.400 --> 00:56:08.789
the crop from that country.

00:56:08.789 --> 00:56:13.440
And the cultural exchange and the one on one
relationship that are developed then have

00:56:13.440 --> 00:56:17.970
very long lasting...because there are the
people who will ultimately be in the leadership

00:56:17.970 --> 00:56:19.190
of those countries.

00:56:19.190 --> 00:56:24.529
And so that's a dividend that you can't overlook
that most people really seem to be unaware

00:56:24.529 --> 00:56:25.529
of.

00:56:25.529 --> 00:56:29.579
John Charles Daly: Well, I very much regret but
our time has run out and this concludes another

00:56:29.579 --> 00:56:35.359
public policy forum presented by the American
Enterprise Institute for Public Policy Research.

00:56:35.359 --> 00:56:43.009
On behalf of AEI our hearty thanks to the
expert panelists, General Brent Scowcroft,

00:56:43.009 --> 00:56:48.079
Representative William L. Dickinson, Senator
Joseph R. Biden Junior and Representative

00:56:48.079 --> 00:56:54.469
Les Aspin and our thanks also to our guests
and experts in the audience for their participation.

00:56:54.469 --> 00:56:56.079
Goodbye from Washington.

